# Words 351-400 (phrasal verbs PHR-101..150)
R = [
("PHR-101","turn into – turned into – turned into – turning into","'Turn into' + noun (turn into ice). For an adjective, use 'turn' alone (turned cold).","TURN INTO: turn around and you're INTO a new shape.","'The frog turned into a prince' — a classic fairy-tale use.","Write a sentence about something that turned into something else.",("water is in the freezer","it gets colder and colder","it becomes ice"),"turn into vs become","Same meaning with nouns. Turn into suggests a complete or surprising change."),
("PHR-102","turn out – turned out – turned out – turning out","A finished event needs the past 'turned'.","TURN OUT: the result TURNS OUT from behind a curtain.","'It turns out (that)…' = we discovered that…","Write a sentence about something that turned out better than expected.",("make a plan","things happen","see the final result"),"turn out vs end up","Turn out = the result is (often surprising). End up = finally be in a place or situation."),
("PHR-103","turn around – turned around – turned around – turning around","Both actions are in the past: 'turned' and 'saw'.","TURN AROUND: spin AROUND to face the other way.","'Turn around a business' = make a failing business succeed.","Write a sentence about a time you turned around and saw a surprise.",("face forward","spin your body","face the other way"),"turn around vs return","Turn around = face the other way. Return = go back to a place."),
("PHR-104","wear out – wore out – worn out – wearing out","'Wear' is irregular: wear – wore – worn.","WEAR → WORE → WORN, like TEAR → TORE → TORN.","'I'm worn out' = I'm extremely tired.","Write a sentence about something you used until it wore out.",("use shoes every day","the soles get thin","they have holes"),"wear out vs wear off","Wear out = become damaged or very tired. Wear off = an effect slowly disappears."),
("PHR-105","wear off – wore off – worn off – wearing off","The present continuous needs -ing (wearing off), and 'medicine's' shows possession.","WEAR OFF: the effect is slowly rubbed OFF.","Common with medicine, anaesthesia, excitement and paint.","Write a sentence about how long a painkiller takes to wear off.",("take a painkiller","the pain goes away","after hours, the pain returns"),"wear off vs wear out","Wear off = an effect fades. Wear out = be used until damaged, or become exhausted."),
("PHR-106","use up – used up – used up – using up","Use the past 'used' and 'ended', and add 'the' before 'month'.","USE UP: use it until it's all gone — the level goes UP to empty.","'Use up leftovers' = finish old food so nothing is wasted.","Write a sentence about something you used up quickly this month.",("have a full pack","keep using it","nothing is left"),"use up vs run out","Use up = you finish it by using. Run out = you find there's none left."),
("PHR-107","come across – came across – come across – coming across","'Last week' needs the past 'came'.","COME ACROSS: walk ACROSS a room and find something by chance.","'He comes across as rude' = he seems rude to others.","Write a sentence about something interesting you came across online.",("look through old boxes","find something by chance","feel surprised"),"come across vs find","Find = discover, often by looking. Come across = find by chance, without looking."),
("PHR-108","come back – came back – come back – coming back","He/she in the simple present needs -s: comes back.","COME BACK: come BACK the way you went.","'Come back soon!' is a warm goodbye.","Write a sentence about what time you come back home every day.",("leave home in the morning","finish work","arrive home again"),"come back vs go back","Come back = return to where the speaker is. Go back = return to another place."),
("PHR-109","come out – came out – come out – coming out","'Yesterday' needs the past 'came'.","COME OUT: step OUT into public view.","A film or book 'comes out' = is released. 'Come out' also means reveal one's sexuality.","Write a sentence about a film or song that came out recently.",("keep news secret","announce it","everyone knows"),"come out vs release","A company releases a product. The product comes out."),
("PHR-110","come along – came along – come along – coming along","The present continuous needs 'is coming along'.","COME ALONG: walk ALONG with someone, or move ahead.","'Come along!' = come with me. 'How's it coming along?' = how's progress?","Write a sentence asking a friend how their project is coming along.",("start a project","make steady progress","it's taking shape"),"come along vs come with","Come along = accompany (no object needed), or make progress. Come with = accompany + person: 'Come with me.'"),
("PHR-111","drop off – dropped off – dropped off – dropping off","Use 'at' with 'drop off', and the person can go in the middle: 'dropped my sister off at'.","DROP OFF: the car stops, and someone drops OFF.","'I dropped off' can also mean I fell asleep.","Write a sentence about who drops you off at school or work.",("drive to the station","stop and let someone out","drive away"),"drop off vs pick up","Opposites: drop off = leave someone at a place. Pick up = collect them."),
("PHR-112","pick out – picked out – picked out – picking out","'Pick out' needs an object: pick one out, pick out a shirt.","PICK OUT: pick one OUT of the crowd.","'Can you pick me out in this photo?' = can you find me?","Write a sentence about something you picked out at a shop.",("see many options","compare them","choose one"),"pick out vs pick up","Pick out = choose. Pick up = lift, or collect."),
("PHR-113","sort through – sorted through – sorted through – sorting through","'Sort through' can't be split; the object comes after 'through'.","SORT THROUGH: go THROUGH a pile, sorting as you go.","Common with papers, emails and old clothes.","Write a sentence about something you need to sort through at home.",("have a messy pile","look at each item","organise them"),"sort through vs sort out","Sort through = examine many items. Sort out = organise or fix."),
("PHR-114","clean up – cleaned up – cleaned up – cleaning up","'Clean up after yourself' uses a reflexive pronoun, because the mess is your own.","CLEAN UP: pick the mess UP off the floor.","'Clean up your act' = start behaving better.","Write a sentence about how you clean up after a party.",("the room is messy","remove the rubbish","the room is clean"),"clean up vs clean","Clean = remove dirt. Clean up = remove mess and make the whole place tidy."),
("PHR-115","tidy up – tidied up – tidied up – tidying up","After 'need', use 'to' + base verb.","TIDY UP: put everything UP in its place.","'Tidy up' is more British; 'clean up' is common in both US and UK.","Write a sentence about when you tidy up your room.",("things are scattered","put each thing in place","the room is neat"),"tidy up vs clean up","Tidy up = put in order. Clean up = remove dirt and mess."),
("PHR-116","fill up – filled up – filled up – filling up","Use 'fill out' or 'fill in' for forms. 'Fill up' is for containers.","FILL UP: the level goes UP to the top.","'Fill her up' at a petrol pump = fill the tank.","Write a sentence about what you fill up before a long drive.",("the tank is empty","pour in fuel","it's full"),"fill up vs fill out","Fill up = make a container full. Fill out = complete a form. (Indian English often says 'fill up a form'.)"),
("PHR-117","fill out – filled out – filled out – filling out","'Fill' alone means make a container full. Forms need 'fill out' or 'fill in'.","FILL OUT: fill OUT every blank line on the form.","'Fill in' (UK) and 'fill out' (US) both work for forms.","Write a sentence about a form you filled out recently.",("get a blank form","write your details","hand it in"),"fill out vs fill in","Same meaning for forms. Fill in is more British; fill out is more American.","Please fill this form.","Please fill out (or fill in) this form."),
("PHR-118","block off – blocked off – blocked off – blocking off","For a plan, use the continuous or 'will': 'are blocking off tomorrow'.","BLOCK OFF: put BLOCKS across so it's OFF limits.","Police 'block off' roads for events and accidents.","Write a sentence about a road that was blocked off near you.",("an event is planned","put up barriers","no one can pass"),"block off vs block","Block = stop or be in the way. Block off = close an area completely on purpose."),
("PHR-119","close down – closed down – closed down – closing down","A finished past event needs the simple past 'closed down'.","CLOSE DOWN: the shutters come DOWN for good.","'Closing-down sale' = a final sale before a shop shuts forever.","Write a sentence about a shop in your area that closed down.",("a business struggles","it stops trading","it shuts forever"),"close down vs close","Close = shut (for the night). Close down = stop operating permanently.","The shop was close down last year.","The shop closed down last year."),
("PHR-120","shut down – shut down – shut down – shutting down","Use 'turn off' for lights and switches; 'shut down' is for systems, machines and businesses.","SHUT DOWN: a computer's power goes DOWN.","'Shut down' also means stop someone rudely: 'He shut me down.'","Write a sentence about how you shut down your computer.",("the computer is running","close programs and power off","the screen is black"),"shut down vs turn off","Turn off = switch off (lights, TV). Shut down = stop a system properly (computer, factory)."),
("PHR-121","break out – broke out – broken out – breaking out","Use 'in' to show where something broke out.","BREAK OUT: something BREAKS OUT suddenly, like a fire from a closed box.","'Break out of prison' = escape. 'Break out in a rash' = skin reaction.","Write a sentence about a fire or fight that broke out in a news story.",("everything is calm","trouble starts suddenly","people react"),"break out vs start","Start is neutral. Break out = start suddenly, usually something bad (war, fire, disease)."),
("PHR-122","break into – broke into – broken into – breaking into","'Break into' + place; 'break in' has no object after it.","BREAK INTO: BREAK the lock and go INTO the house.","'Break into a smile' = suddenly smile.","Write a sentence about how to protect a house from being broken into.",("thieves reach the house","force the door","enter illegally"),"break into vs break in","Break into a house (object). Thieves broke in (no object)."),
("PHR-123","break up with – broke up with – broken up with – breaking up with","'With' is needed before the person.","BREAK UP WITH: the relationship breaks, WITH one person leaving.","Used for couples, and sometimes for bands and groups.","Write a neutral sentence using 'break up with'.",("a couple has problems","one says it's over","they separate"),"break up with vs break up","Break up WITH a person. 'They broke up' (no object) is also correct."),
("PHR-124","ease off – eased off – eased off – easing off","Use 'ease off ON' a person or topic, not 'to'.","EASE OFF: take your foot OFF the pressure pedal.","'The rain eased off' = it became lighter.","Write a sentence asking someone to ease off on the pressure.",("press hard","slowly reduce the pressure","things feel lighter"),"ease off vs stop","Stop = end completely. Ease off = reduce gradually."),
("PHR-125","ease up – eased up – eased up – easing up","Use 'ease up ON' a person, not 'to'.","EASE UP: loosen UP your grip.","'Ease up!' = relax, don't be so hard.","Write a sentence about a teacher who eased up on the class before exams.",("be very strict","become more relaxed","people feel better"),"ease up vs ease off","Almost the same. Ease up is used more for strictness; ease off for pressure or effort."),
("PHR-126","speed up – sped up – sped up – speeding up","'Speed' is irregular: the past is 'sped'.","SPEED → SPED, like FEED → FED.","'Can we speed things up?' = can we go faster?","Write a sentence about something you would like to speed up.",("move slowly","press the accelerator","move faster"),"speed up vs hurry up","Speed up = go faster (vehicles, processes). Hurry up = move faster because you're late (people)."),
("PHR-127","slow down – slowed down – slowed down – slowing down","The -s goes on the verb: 'slows down'.","SLOW DOWN: the speed number goes DOWN.","'Slow down!' is also advice to relax and not rush life.","Write a sentence about when drivers should slow down.",("drive fast","press the brake gently","move slowly"),"slow down vs stop","Slow down = reduce speed. Stop = reach zero speed."),
("PHR-128","add up – added up – added up – adding up","'Add up' needs numbers or amounts as the object.","ADD UP: stack the numbers UP into a total.","'It doesn't add up' = something seems wrong or illogical.","Write a sentence about adding up your monthly expenses.",("have a list of numbers","add them one by one","get the total"),"add up vs add","Add = put one thing to another. Add up = find the total of several numbers."),
("PHR-129","add on – added on – added on – adding on","'They' + past time needs the past 'added'.","ADD ON: stick something extra ON.","'Add-on' (noun) = an extra feature you pay more for.","Write a sentence about an extra charge a shop added on.",("have a basic price","include something extra","the price goes up"),"add on vs add","Add = put together. Add on = attach something extra to what already exists.","They add on extra charges yesterday.","They added on extra charges yesterday."),
("PHR-130","act up – acted up – acted up – acting up","With 'since', use the present perfect continuous.","ACT UP: a machine ACTS badly, UP to mischief.","Used for children behaving badly and machines not working well.","Write a sentence about a device that has been acting up.",("the phone works fine","it starts freezing","it doesn't work properly"),"act up vs break down","Act up = not work properly, on and off. Break down = stop working completely."),
("PHR-131","act out – acted out – acted out – acting out","After 'by', use the -ing form.","ACT OUT: ACT the story OUT in front of people.","'Act out' also means show bad feelings through bad behaviour.","Write a sentence about a story your class acted out.",("read a story","play the characters","the audience watches"),"act out vs act","Act = perform, or behave. Act out = perform a story, or show feelings through behaviour."),
("PHR-132","blow up – blew up – blown up – blowing up","'Blow' is irregular: blow – blew – blown.","BLOW → BLEW → BLOWN, like GROW → GREW → GROWN.","'Blow up a balloon' = fill it with air. 'Blow up a photo' = enlarge it.","Write a sentence about a time someone blew up in anger.",("feel angry inside","the anger builds","shout suddenly"),"blow up vs explode","Explode = burst violently. Blow up = explode, or become very angry, or fill with air."),
("PHR-133","burn out – burned/burnt out – burned/burnt out – burning out","After 'am', use the adjective form 'burned out' / 'burnt out'.","BURN OUT: a candle burns until it goes OUT.","'Burnout' (noun) = exhaustion from overwork.","Write a sentence about how to avoid burning out at work.",("work too many hours","feel more tired each day","have no energy left"),"burn out vs tired","Tired = needs rest. Burned out = exhausted for a long time from stress and overwork."),
("PHR-134","burn down – burned/burnt down – burned/burnt down – burning down","After 'was', use the past participle 'burned'.","BURN DOWN: the building falls DOWN in fire.","Used for whole buildings, not small objects.","Write a sentence about fire safety to stop a house burning down.",("a fire starts","it spreads through the house","only ashes remain"),"burn down vs burn","Burn = be on fire, or damage by fire. Burn down = destroy a whole building by fire."),
("PHR-135","dry out – dried out – dried out – drying out","Use 'in the sun', not 'on the sun'.","DRY OUT: the water goes OUT of the cloth.","Also used for skin, bread and soil: 'Don't let the soil dry out.'","Write a sentence about how clothes dry out in the monsoon.",("clothes are wet","hang them in the sun","they are completely dry"),"dry out vs dry up","Dry out = become completely dry (clothes, skin). Dry up = a source runs out (a river, money)."),
("PHR-136","dry up – dried up – dried up – drying up","A finished past event needs the simple past: 'dried up'.","DRY UP: a river's water goes UP into the sky.","'My ideas dried up' = I had no more ideas.","Write a sentence about a river or pond that dried up.",("a river flows","no rain for months","the riverbed is empty"),"dry up vs dry out","Dry up = a supply disappears. Dry out = something wet becomes dry."),
("PHR-137","freeze up – froze up – frozen up – freezing up","He/she/it in the simple present needs -s: freezes up.","FREEZE → FROZE → FROZEN, like CHOOSE → CHOSE → CHOSEN.","'I froze up in the interview' = I couldn't speak from nerves.","Write a sentence about a time you froze up in front of people.",("stand in front of people","feel nervous","can't speak or move"),"freeze up vs hang","Both describe a computer that stops. Freeze up is also used for people too nervous to act."),
("PHR-138","heat up – heated up – heated up – heating up","A past time needs 'heated', and 'ago' doesn't take 'in'.","HEAT UP: the temperature goes UP.","'The debate heated up' = it became more intense.","Write a sentence about how you heat up leftover food.",("the food is cold","put it in the microwave","it's hot"),"heat up vs warm up","Heat up = make hot. Warm up = make a little warm, or prepare for exercise."),
("PHR-139","cool down – cooled down – cooled down – cooling down","'Cool down' needs no object here, and 'of' is wrong.","COOL DOWN: the temperature — or temper — goes DOWN.","After exercise, 'cool down' = stretch slowly to relax your muscles.","Write a sentence about what you do to cool down after an argument.",("feel hot or angry","rest and breathe","feel calm"),"cool down vs calm down","Calm down = stop being upset. Cool down = become less hot, or less angry.","I need to cool down of my anger.","I need to cool down."),
("PHR-140","chill out – chilled out – chilled out – chilling out","'Chill out' needs no 'yourself'.","CHILL OUT: let the heat of stress OUT and feel chilled.","Informal only — avoid it in formal emails.","Write a casual sentence about how you chill out on Sundays.",("feel stressed","relax with friends or music","feel easy"),"chill out vs relax","Same meaning. Chill out is informal slang."),
("PHR-141","log in – logged in – logged in – logging in","Use 'log in TO' + the account or site. 'Log into' is also used.","LOG IN: your name goes IN to the system's log book.","'Login' (one word) is the noun: 'your login details'.","Write a sentence about which app you log in to every day.",("open the website","type your username and password","your account opens"),"log in vs sign in","Same meaning. Sign in is often used on consumer apps."),
("PHR-142","log out – logged out – logged out – logging out","After 'forgot', use 'to' + verb; and 'log out OF', not 'from'.","LOG OUT: your name goes OUT of the system.","Always log out on shared computers to keep accounts safe.","Write a sentence reminding someone to log out of a shared computer.",("finish your work","click Log out","the account closes"),"log out vs log off","Same meaning. Log off is slightly older."),
("PHR-143","sign up – signed up – signed up – signing up","Use 'sign up FOR' something, not 'to'.","SIGN UP: put your SIGNature UP on the list.","'Sign up' (register) vs 'sign in' (enter an account you already have).","Write a sentence about a course or service you signed up for.",("see a new course","fill in your details","you are registered"),"sign up vs sign in","Sign up = create a new account or register. Sign in = enter an account you already have."),
("PHR-144","sign in – signed in – signed in – signing in","Use 'sign in TO' + the app or site.","SIGN IN: write your name IN the book at reception.","Offices: 'Please sign in at reception.'","Write a sentence about signing in at a building's reception.",("arrive at the office","write your name or log in","you're recorded as present"),"sign in vs sign up","Sign in = enter an existing account. Sign up = register for the first time."),
("PHR-145","sign out – signed out – signed out – signing out","Use 'sign out OF' + the account.","SIGN OUT: your name goes OUT of the system.","'Sign out a book' = record that you're borrowing it.","Write a sentence about when you sign out of your work email.",("finish using the account","click Sign out","the account is closed"),"sign out vs log out","Same meaning. Sign out is common on apps; log out on older systems."),
("PHR-146","opt out – opted out – opted out – opting out","Use 'opt out OF', not 'from'.","OPT OUT: choose ('opt') to stay OUT.","Emails and apps: 'You can opt out at any time.'","Write a sentence about a service or email list you opted out of.",("get added to a list","click Unsubscribe","you're no longer included"),"opt out vs opt in","Opt in = choose to join. Opt out = choose not to join, or to leave."),
("PHR-147","drop out – dropped out – dropped out – dropping out","Use the past 'dropped' and 'drop out OF', not 'from'.","DROP OUT: fall OUT of the race before the finish line.","'Dropout' (noun) = a student who left before finishing.","Write a neutral sentence about why some students drop out of school.",("join a course","stop attending","leave without finishing"),"drop out vs quit","Quit = stop doing anything. Drop out = leave school or a course before finishing."),
("PHR-148","hold on – held on – held on – holding on","'In five minutes' = after five minutes from now; 'minute' needs -s.","HOLD ON: HOLD the phone line ON — don't hang up.","'Hold on tight!' = grip firmly.","Write a sentence asking someone to hold on while you check something.",("someone calls you","ask them to wait","come back to the call"),"hold on vs wait","Same meaning. Hold on is informal and common on the phone."),
("PHR-149","hold up – held up – held up – holding up","After 'was', use the past participle 'held'.","HOLD → HELD, like the traffic HELD you UP.","'Hold up' also means rob with a weapon: 'a bank hold-up'.","Write a sentence about something that held you up this morning.",("leave on time","get stuck in traffic","arrive late"),"hold up vs delay","Same meaning. Hold up is informal; delay is neutral."),
("PHR-150","hold back – held back – held back – holding back","'During the speech' is past, so use 'held'.","HOLD BACK: HOLD your tears BACK inside.","'Don't hold back' = say or do everything; don't be shy.","Write a sentence about a time you held back your feelings.",("feel strong emotion","stop yourself from showing it","stay calm outside"),"hold back vs hide","Hide = keep out of sight. Hold back = stop yourself from showing or doing something."),
]
